<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Westminster Abbey</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Westminster Abbey is a church in Westminster, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a> and consecrated in 1065, then rebuilt again in Gothic style from 1245 under Henry III. Every English and British monarch since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>, crowned there on Christmas Day 1066, has been crowned in the abbey, except Edward V and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-viii">Edward VIII</a>, who were never crowned. Many kings, queens and notable figures are buried there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey.jpg" alt="Westminster Abbey" width="320" height="643" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antiquary · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Westminster_Abbey,_Westminster.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
